== 1. What the last red test said

    $ gh api repos/chao2hang/chaos-code/actions/jobs/111285204201 \
          --jq '{conclusion,steps:[.steps[]|{number,name,conclusion}]}'

    thread 'implementations::lsp::manager::tests::a_header_decodes_the_uri_instead_of_cutting_the_scheme_off'
    panicked at crates\codegen\xai-grok-tools\src\implementations\lsp\manager.rs:817:9:
    the reader was handed the escaped form: file:///dir/a%20b.cs

    test result: FAILED. 3116 passed; 1 failed; 2 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out

The whole `xai-grok-tools` step, summed over its seven `test result:` lines, was
3135 passed / 1 failed / 16 ignored. The message is the test's own: it hands
`append_file` the URI `file:///dir/a%20b.cs` and asserts the header it produces is not the
escaped form. On Windows it was the escaped form, unchanged, because the URI came back as
the fallback rather than as a path at all.

== 2. Why only Windows could fail it

`CollectedDiagnostics::append_file` builds the header through one helper
(`crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/lsp/manager.rs:90`):

    let display_path = super::path_for_file_uri(uri)
        .map(|path| path.display().to_string())
        .unwrap_or_else(|| uri.to_owned());

and the helper is one expression (`crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/lsp/mod.rs:75`):

    pub fn path_for_file_uri(uri: &str) -> Option<PathBuf> {
        Url::parse(uri).ok()?.to_file_path().ok()
    }

`to_file_path()` on a Windows target requires the first path segment to be a drive letter.
In `url` 2.5.8, `file_url_segments_to_pathbuf_windows` (src/lib.rs:3128) accepts a first
segment of length 2 (`C:`) or length 4 (`C%3A`) and everything else reaches the catch-all
arm at src/lib.rs:3164:

    _ => return Err(()),

`file:///dir/a%20b.cs` has no drive: its first segment is `dir`, three characters, so on
Windows the conversion fails, `path_for_file_uri` returns `None`, and `append_file` shows
the URI exactly as it arrived. That is the shipped behaviour and it is the right one - a
URI naming no file on the host should be shown rather than mangled - but it means the test
was asserting a Windows impossibility. On Linux the same URI converts to `/dir/a b.cs` and
the test passes.

== 3. What the test was supposed to protect, and did not

The defect this test was written against (`maintenance-line-review-2026-10-04.log` section 10)
was slicing `file://` off a stored URI. The Windows half of that defect is
`file:///C:/dir/file.ts` becoming `/C:/dir/file.ts`, a path that resolves under the root of
whatever drive is current. The test as written never fed that URI to the code: it used a
drive-less one. So on the platform whose bug it was written for, it checked a case that
cannot occur, and the case that does occur went unchecked on both platforms.

== 4. The rewrite

Three tests replace one, in `crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/lsp/manager.rs`.
The first builds its URI from a path the host can name, so the fixture is a real document
URI on whichever platform runs it, and compares the header against that same path:

    let drive = if cfg!(windows) { "C:" } else { "" };
    let path = PathBuf::from(format!("{drive}{sep}dir{sep}a b.cs"));
    let uri = super::file_uri(&path).expect("a native path makes a file uri");

`file_uri` is the shipped inverse, so the fixture cannot drift from what the rest of the
crate stores, and the test asserts on equality with a native path rather than on a suffix,
which is what let the mixed-separator bug through elsewhere (`join_posix_relative`,
`model-path-drive-less-root-2026-10-03.log` section 9). It also asserts the fixture really
arrives escaped, so a future `file_uri` that stopped escaping cannot silently empty the test.

The second test feeds the Windows form the first one now cannot use - `file:///C:/dir/a%20b.cs` -
and asserts only what holds on both hosts: no `%20`, no `file:`, and the header ending in
`C:{sep}dir{sep}a b.cs`. On Windows that is the whole path; on Linux it is what the drive-less
conversion of a Windows URI yields here, and the escapes still have to be gone.

The third test pins the fallback that produced the CI message: a URI that names no file
anywhere (`untitled:Untitled-1%20a.cs`) is shown as it arrived.

