== 6. Windows verification without a Windows machine

The container in `docs/verification/model-path-windows-probe.Dockerfile` carries a mingw
toolchain and wine; the same image the drive-less-root batch used was rebuilt by its own
Dockerfile and given the cross-run environment the previous batch recorded:

    $ docker build -t chaos-winprobe:local -f docs/verification/model-path-windows-probe.Dockerfile docs/verification
    $ docker run --rm -v <repo>:/work -v {SCRATCH}:/tools -v ~/.cargo:/root/.cargo \
          -v winprobe-target:/tmp/tw -w /work -e CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/tmp/tw \
          -e CARGO_NET_OFFLINE=true \
          -e CARGO_TARGET_X86_64_PC_WINDOWS_GNU_LINKER=x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc \
          -e CC_x86_64_pc_windows_gnu=x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc \
          -e CXX_x86_64_pc_windows_gnu=x86_64-w64-mingw32-g++ \
          -e AR_x86_64_pc_windows_gnu=x86_64-w64-mingw32-ar \
          chaos-winprobe:local bash {SCRATCH}/winprobe/header-run.sh

That script builds the `bcryptprimitives.dll` shim the gnu target needs (wine 8.0 does not
export `ProcessPrng`, see `model-path-drive-less-root-2026-10-03.log` section 7), installs a
cargo runner that copies the shim next to each test executable and execs
`/usr/lib/wine/wine64`, then runs the filter three times: as committed, with HEAD's file put
back verbatim, and after the restore.

    ---- baseline, tests as committed: exit 0
        running 8 tests
        test result: ok. 8 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 3113 filtered out
    ---- HEAD's version of the test, the one CI ran: exit 101
        running 6 tests
        thread 'implementations::lsp::manager::tests::a_header_decodes_the_uri_instead_of_cutting_the_scheme_off'
        (256) panicked at crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/lsp/manager.rs:817:9:
        the reader was handed the escaped form: file:///dir/a%20b.cs
        test result: FAILED. 5 passed; 1 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 3113 filtered out
    ---- restored tree: exit 0
        running 8 tests
        test result: ok. 8 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 3113 filtered out
    restored byte-identically: True

So the Windows failure is not inferred from a CI log here: it was produced on a Windows
target, with the same panic line (`manager.rs:817:9`) and the same message the GitHub runner
printed, and the rewritten tests were then run green on that same target. The file the run
restores is the one the run started from, compared byte for byte.

Caveat, the same one recorded by the batch that built the image: a wine prefix is not
Windows. It is Debian's wine 8.0. Path arithmetic, percent decoding and `PathBuf` display
strings are what this test exercises and none of them depend on NTFS semantics, real access
control or a real per-process drive state, but the final verdict on the leg is still the CI
`platform tests (windows-latest)` step.

== 7. Non-vacuity, on the host

Each mutation is an exact-string edit to shipped source, one test binary, then a restore
compared byte for byte and given a fresh mtime so cargo cannot reuse the mutated binary.
Driver: `{SCRATCH}/mutate-header-decode.py`, transcript
`{SCRATCH}/mutate-header-decode.log`. Baseline and restored tree both `rc=0`, no failures.

    KILLED H1 append_file slices the scheme off (the original bug): rc=101
        a_header_decodes_the_uri_instead_of_cutting_the_scheme_off :: assertion `left == right` failed:
            the header is not the native path the reader has to open
            left: "/dir/a%20b.cs" right: "/dir/a b.cs"
        a_windows_document_uri_arrives_decoded_and_without_its_scheme :: the reader was handed
            the escaped form: /C:/dir/a%20b.cs
    KILLED H2 path_for_file_uri hand-strips instead of parsing: rc=101
        (same two tests, same two messages)
    KILLED H3 a uri that is not a path here collapses to nothing: rc=101
        a_uri_that_names_no_file_is_left_alone :: assertion `left == right` failed
            left: ":" right: "untitled:Untitled-1%20a.cs:"

H1 is the defect as it shipped. H2 is the half-fix that decodes by hand. H3 is the
degenerate fallback (`unwrap_or_default`), and it is the only one of the three that the
third test can see: that test exists to make the fallback observable, which is exactly the
behaviour the Windows leg produced by accident.

== 8. Two false results this batch produced before it produced a real one

The first wine run reported that HEAD's test passed on the Windows target. It had not run
HEAD's test at all: `shutil.copy2` puts the *source* file's mtime on the destination, cargo
rebuilds only when a source is newer than the last build, and the HEAD copy was older than
the build it was meant to be measured against, so cargo re-ran the new binary and everything
looked green. The documented trap in `docs/ci-test-debt.md` is about restoring; this was the
same trap on the mutating side of a mutation round. Fix: touch the file forward after both
the mutation and the restore.

What caught it was not a verdict but a count. The driver asserts that the number of tests
libtest says it ran equals the number of `#[test]` attributes in the file it just wrote -
8 for the rewritten file, 6 for HEAD's. A stale binary cannot fake a test count. That guard
is worth keeping in any driver that swaps a source file out and rebuilds: `test result: ok`
is a claim about the binary that ran, not about the file on disk.

The second false result was the driver's own crash: the count was parsed out of
`test result: ok. 8 passed` by position, and position 2 of that line is `ok.`, so
`int()` raised. The parse now reads libtest's `running 8 tests` line, which counts what ran
rather than what passed, and would also have caught the filtered-out case.

== 9. Left open

- Whether the Windows leg is green is decided by the run this push triggers. This batch
  changes one test file and the expectation is `3116 passed; 0 failed`; the count of
  ignored tests moves by two because two tests were added.
- The Windows implementation of a persistent shell (`TODO.md`, the `persistent_shell` row)
  is still not done; only the silent-ignore half was closed.
- `#[cfg(windows)]`-gated code in these crates is still compiled by CI only; the wine run
  executes tests that compile for `x86_64-pc-windows-gnu` and those are not all the same
  tests, because some of them are gated `#[cfg(windows)]` and some spawn `python3`, which
  wine cannot execute. The LSP mock-server family was therefore not re-run under wine.

